        "id": "ch7_3_5-3",
        "text": "myqueue.put(item)\nprocesses = []\nfor id in range(PROCESSES_POOL_SIZE):\np = Process (target= mydownloader , args=(myqueue,))\np.daemon = True\np.start()\nstart_time = time.monotonic()\nmyqueue.join()\ntotal_exec_time = time.monotonic() - start_time\nprint(f'Time taken to download: {total_exec_time:.2f}\nseconds')\nif __name__ == '__main__':\nmain()\n266      Multiprocessing, Multithreading, and Asynchronous Programming\nWe ran this application by varying the different number of processes, such as 3 , 5 , 7 , and\n10 . We found that the time it took to download the same files (as for the case study of\nmultithreading) is slightly better than with the multithreaded application. The execution\ntime will vary from machine to machine, but on our machine (MacBook Pro: Intel Core i5\nwith 16 GB RAM), it took around 5 seconds with 5 processes and 3 seconds with 10\nprocesses running in parallel. This improvement of 1 second over the multithreaded\napplication is in line with the expected results as multiprocessing provides\ntrue concurrency.",
